 ## Overview
-Ambient Occlusion via SDF approximates the darkening of crevices and contact points by sampling the signed distance function (SDF) along the surface normal [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It detects occlusion by comparing the actual distance to nearby geometry against the expected distance if the surface were isolated; a smaller actual distance indicates an obstacle is closer, reducing the ambient light contribution [S2, S5, unofficial]. Unlike screen-space methods or shadow maps, this technique requires no separate rendering pass or auxiliary buffers, relying solely on the scene's distance function [S6, unofficial]. It is computationally cheaper than full ray-marched shadows but provides a local approximation of global illumination effects [S4, unofficial].
+Ambient Occlusion via SDF approximates the darkening of crevices and contact points by sampling the signed distance function (SDF) along the surface normal [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It detects occlusion by comparing the actual distance to nearby geometry against the expected distance if the surface were isolated; a smaller actual distance indicates an obstacle is closer, reducing the ambient light contribution [S2, S5, unofficial]. Unlike screen-space methods or shadow maps, this technique requires no separate rendering pass, relying solely on the scene's distance function sampled differently [S6, unofficial]. It is computationally cheaper than full ray-marched shadows but provides a local approximation of global illumination effects [S4, unofficial].
 
